WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.841
The first step in secrets management is knowing what counts as a secret.

2
00:00:04.841 --> 00:00:06.185
Common .

3
00:00:06.185 --> 00:00:21.850
NET secret categories include connection strings, database credentials, API keys, certificates, private keys, signing keys, encryption keys, access tokens, refresh tokens, webhook secrets, storage credentials, and service identities.

4
00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.548
A value does not need to look dramatic to be important.

5
00:00:25.548 --> 00:00:29.515
Some values are sensitive even when they are not passwords.

6
00:00:29.515 --> 00:00:35.767
A connection string may reveal internal names, trust assumptions, or a managed identity path.

7
00:00:35.767 --> 00:00:39.599
A certificate name may point to important trust material.

8
00:00:39.599 --> 00:00:44.978
A tenant identifier such as ExampleTenantId may affect routing or authorization.

9
00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:50.827
A feature mode may change security behavior even if it does not grant access by itself.

10
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:54.256
Secret references are different from secret values.

11
00:00:54.256 --> 00:01:03.198
A configuration key that says to use ExampleCertificateName or ExampleStorageAccount may be safe to document when it is only a label.

12
00:01:03.198 --> 00:01:09.652
The actual certificate, signing key, storage credential, or token belongs in a protected system.

13
00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:15.568
Mixing references and values makes reviews harder and accidental disclosure more likely.

14
00:01:15.568 --> 00:01:27.401
Each secret should have inventory data: owning team, application, environment, purpose, allowed readers, rotation expectations, expiration if applicable, and emergency contact.

15
00:01:27.401 --> 00:01:35.065
Without ownership, secrets become permanent background dependencies that nobody feels responsible for maintaining.

16
00:01:35.065 --> 00:01:46.428
Unmanaged secrets often hide in old appsettings files, scripts, documents, tickets, screenshots, pasted chat messages, sample projects, and forgotten test configuration.

17
00:01:46.428 --> 00:01:52.008
These locations are not designed for lifecycle control, access review, or rotation.

18
00:01:52.008 --> 00:01:57.588
Cleanup should include collaboration tools and documentation, not only source code.

19
00:01:57.588 --> 00:02:01.689
Sensitive configuration should be reviewed alongside secrets.

20
00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:14.060
A setting that disables a security check, points to a test integration, changes token validation behavior, or relaxes diagnostics can create risk even if it is not itself a credential.

21
00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:19.976
The inventory should capture both access-granting secrets and high-impact configuration.
